{"id":"675d7f8a-3c48-4771-8585-0145aac11547","object":"response","created_at":1793285187,"status":"completed","background":false,"completed_at":1793285191,"content_filters":null,"error":null,"frequency_penalty":0.0,"incomplete_details":null,"instructions":null,"max_output_tokens":8000,"max_tool_calls":null,"model":"deepseek-flash","moderation":null,"output":[{"type":"message","id":"012a1c5e-0873-4577-8362-552a97f76a53","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"output_text","annotations":[],"logprobs":[],"text":"{\"paragraphs\":[{\"key\":\"overview\",\"text\":\"This frozen example illustrates head insertion in a singly linked list under an abstract allocation model. The specification supplies an initial value of 9 and two insertion values, 1 then 2, with both allocation attempts marked successful. The trace begins with a single node i0 holding the initial value and a null next pointer, and the caller head initially points at i0.\",\"fact_ids\":[\"trace_step_00\"]},{\"key\":\"first_insertion\",\"text\":\"On the first call, the allocation phase introduces a fresh node t0 whose data and next fields are initially indeterminate. The data phase assigns the first insertion value to t0, and the link phase points t0's next field at the existing head i0. The function then returns t0, and the caller head becomes t0, so the list now begins with the newly inserted node followed by i0.\",\"fact_ids\":[\"trace_step_01\",\"trace_step_02\",\"trace_step_03\",\"trace_step_04\",\"trace_step_05\"]},{\"key\":\"second_insertion\",\"text\":\"On the second call, a fresh node t1 is allocated with indeterminate fields. Its data field is set to the second insertion value, and its next field is linked to the current head t0. The function returns t1, and the caller head is updated to t1, placing the second inserted node at the front of the list.\",\"fact_ids\":[\"trace_step_06\",\"trace_step_07\",\"trace_step_08\",\"trace_step_09\",\"trace_step_10\"]},{\"key\":\"final_observation\",\"text\":\"The final caller head is the most recently allocated node. Following the known links from that head yields the value prefix 2, then 1, then 9, before the traversal reaches a null pointer. Because the traversal terminates rather than repeating or becoming indeterminate, the status is terminated, and no allocation attempts failed. The reported prefix is the known value prefix, not a claim about any further or exact full traversal.\",\"fact_ids\":[\"obs_caller_head\",\"obs_reachable_prefix\",\"obs_traversal_status\",\"obs_allocation_failures\"]}],\"questions\":{\"caller_head\":{\"stem\":\"What is the final caller head node ID, or none if it is a null pointer?\",\"explanation\":\"After both successful insertions, the caller head is updated to the node returned by the second call, which is the newly allocated node t1.\",\"fact_ids\":[\"obs_caller_head\"]},\"traversal_status\":{\"stem\":\"How is the traversal from the final caller head classified?\",\"explanation\":\"Following the known links from the final head reaches a null pointer without a repeated or indeterminate pointer, so the traversal is classified as terminated rather than cycle or indeterminate.\",\"fact_ids\":[\"obs_traversal_status\"]},\"reachable_prefix\":{\"stem\":\"What is the known integer value prefix reachable from the final caller head before the first repeated or indeterminate pointer?\",\"explanation\":\"The final head is the second inserted node, whose value is 2; its next pointer leads to the first inserted node with value 1, and that node's next pointer leads to the initial node with value 9. This is the known prefix, not an exact full traversal claim.\",\"fact_ids\":[\"obs_reachable_prefix\"]},\"allocation_failures\":{\"stem\":\"How many unsuccessful synthetic allocation attempts occurred?\",\"explanation\":\"Both allocation attempts in the specification are marked successful, so the count of unsuccessful allocation attempts is zero.\",\"fact_ids\":[\"obs_allocation_failures\"]}}}"}],"phase":"final